Portless can assign an app port already occupied on 127.0.0.1 due to hostless free-port probe
Portless version: 0.12.0
OS: macOS

Observed behavior:
Portless assigned an app port that was already listening on IPv4 loopback.

Example:
Portless printed:

PORT=4163 HOST=127.0.0.1 ...

But `lsof` showed:

node ... TCP 127.0.0.1:4163 (LISTEN)

The child process could still start a health server, presumably via a hostless / IPv6 bind, but Portless proxies to `127.0.0.1:<port>`, so readiness checks hit the stale IPv4 listener instead of the new child.

Relevant implementation detail:
The free-port check appears to use `net.createServer().listen(port)` without a host, but the proxy later forwards to `hostname: "127.0.0.1"`.

Expected behavior:
Portless should only assign a port if the same target it proxies to is free, e.g. `127.0.0.1:<port>`. Ideally it should also account for IPv6 loopback or otherwise make the bind/proxy host consistent.

Impact:
This causes confusing readiness failures and orphaned child processes in process managers because the child may appear started while Portless routes to a different process.
